Melting was carried out in a medium-frequency induction furnace with a capacity of nearly 20 kg with an acidic lining. CO 2  process was used to produce standard Y-blocks under ASTM  A536-84  [ 37 ] ( Fig. 1 
                      ). To reduce the thickness and modify the cast structure, hot rolling was performed through four passes at 1050 °C. This decreased the cross-sectional area by 58 %, leading to a size of 400 × 57 × 95 mm. Cylindrical specimens with a diameter of 10 mm were produced through wire-cutting to evaluate the austempered microstructures, and rectangular specimens with a size of 120 × 12 × 7 mm were produced to be rolled to evaluate mechanical properties. To obtain a uniform chemical composition and remove the hot-rolled microstructure, the specimens were placed within a tube furnace protected from oxidation through argon blowing for 2 h at 1050 °C followed by cooling in the furnace.  Table 1 
                       represents the chemical composition of the hot-rolled homogenized alloy.
